resources/python
resources/python_aarch64
# The staged runtime trees `pnpm setup-python` writes: the interpreter and the
# uv release, one directory per architecture, all of them build inputs the
# packaging step copies into `Resources` (see src/shared/bundled-runtime-layout.json).
resources/uv
resources/uv_aarch64

*storybook.log
node_modules

# The built Storybook. `pnpm build-storybook` writes it so a capture run can be
# served from a static tree rather than a dev server, which is the cheap way to
# capture on a loaded machine. It is a build artifact of `src/` - nothing reads
# it, `capture-evidence.mjs` takes an origin and does not care how it is served,
# and committing it put 327 files and ~67k lines of vendored bundle in a docs
# commit (review round 3, MAJOR 1).
storybook-static/

# A machine-local Storybook config used when the preview has to be served from a
# variant of `.storybook/` (currently `reactDocgen: false`, because
# react-docgen-typescript 2.2.2 reads a TypeScript 7 API that no longer exists).
# Nothing in the repo references it - no script or package.json passes
# `-c .storybook-local` - and its `stories` globs are absolute paths into one
# checkout, so tracking it ships one machine's layout in a shared repo and
# contradicts the branch's own provenance records, which describe it as
# untracked (review round 4 addendum, MAJOR).
.storybook-local/

# A throwaway esbuild bundle a test writes next to itself and unlinks. The
# pattern is here because a killed run leaves the file behind and one such file
# was committed at 12MB (`scripts/_interrupt-composer.bundle.*`) - invisible to
# every test, cited by nothing, and a repeat of a build tree a previous review
# round already filed. Names starting with `_` are the convention for these.
# A macOS AppleDouble sidecar: metadata about a sibling file, written when the volume
# cannot store the extended attributes. `scripts/._mark-all-read-control.bundle.mjs`
# is one such - 340KB beside a bundle that the two patterns above already ignore,
# untracked rather than ignored, so a `git add -A` in a tree that has just run the
# desktop suite would commit it (design round 9 filed it twice, once per pass that
# ran that suite). The pattern is the macOS one rather than a list of the names seen
# so far, because the pass that produces one has no say in which name it lands on.
._*

scripts/_*.bundle.mjs
scripts/_*.bundle.cjs
